#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634} :activity/id #uuid "4417fe29-8f51-5120-ac66-9aaeadadb6f3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075034 :tutorial/content "We can find **equivalent ratios** by multiplying or dividing all numbers in a ratio by the same value. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following ratio:\n\n$$\n2:6\n$$\n\nIf we multiply *both* numbers by $5,$ for example, we get the ratio $10:30.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,5 &  \\\\ \\,10 &  & : &  & \\,30 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios $2:6$ and $10:30$ are equivalent. \n\nSimilarly, if we take the ratio $2:6$ and divide *both* numbers by $2,$ we get $1:3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓÷\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratios  $2:6$ and  $1:3$ are also equivalent." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc2c9ce9-c2b0-5d68-81db-b8b0273407f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6aa20fa4-3163-5025-84f7-a1ddd4324cd0" :step/math-academy-id 186681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075035 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the following two ratios are equivalent:\n\n![](images/c6/c673bfcd099ef612f48bd0da7f8535cb9406d23088392e056884fb9415bb5100.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing number?\n\nTo find a ratio equivalent to $3 : 5,$ we multiply or divide both numbers by the same value. \n\nIn this case, we need to get $12$ from ${\\color{blue}{3}},$ so we multiply ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ by $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,↓\\times \\,4 &  \\\\ 12 &  & : &  & \\,\\boxed{\\text{[math]\\phantom{0}[/math]}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to get ![](images/50/50f3c1b8dc3b1628130d1de11abcc455fc22006f32b7486f658c8134fcc3221c.svg) from ${\\color{red}{5}},$ we multiply ${\\color{red}{5}}$ by $4$ as well: \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\times 4 = \\bbox[3px,lightgray]{\\color{red}20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $20.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3539d419-9d5a-5467-8a88-7ef318329859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8204 :tutorial/title "Finding Missing Values in Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "4854b84c-59a0-5a98-a9c6-9b8427de8ab8" :step/math-academy-id 186682 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073447 :question/id #uuid "a453e80d-016f-5a6c-9890-705463eb1fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13120"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ac1e54-c949-5ffe-b377-b0e35240a13c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050924}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518125}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "55be2711-44d0-588a-899f-562ac0853236" :step/math-academy-id 186683 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075036 :tutorial/content "We can simplify ratios by dividing both numbers in the ratio by a common factor.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's simplify the following ratio: \n\n$$\n18:9\n$$\n\nThe numbers $18$ and $9$ share a common factor of $9,$ so we can divide both numbers by $9$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,18 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $18:9$ is equivalent to $2:1.$ \n\nSince $2$ and $1$ have no common factors, $2:1$ is the simplest form of this ratio. We often prefer to work with ratios in their simplest form, where possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ece136-e063-594d-b25d-4cf19bca7890" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8205 :tutorial/title "Expressing Ratios in Their Simplest Form"} :step/id #uuid "23872027-241e-5f61-8492-76236e405360" :step/math-academy-id 186686 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073448 :question/id #uuid "0a57949c-efc9-5403-9e7b-08346153f184" :question/math-academy-id "e-13121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae17eb3f-f444-5ab3-82fd-6c92b97b0e41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515552}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Ratios"} :step/id #uuid "092ffde3-3422-5950-9ca5-9b76fcb363ff" :step/math-academy-id 186687 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073449 :question/id #uuid "dd86c5a5-b8be-5d19-a477-dcf46683881f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13123"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d16202af-d3ac-54bc-9c76-25947a7a0b60" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Statement"} :step/id #uuid "36a366b2-6119-57ed-87f3-fd57834dc2d6" :step/math-academy-id 186690}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073972}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342} :activity/id #uuid "34509cbd-152a-5cd3-b3bc-231dc0557f02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311f524f-5dec-5e52-ac4c-9fb446367ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} {:answer/id #uuid "640e11a1-45f1-5bd9-ba70-d2bd9afff6a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489750} {:answer/id #uuid "6b8d1a24-f0b3-5957-bac1-4b551d6b4aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489770} {:answer/id #uuid "736365bf-c5ec-516a-96d3-5dcf4aad4bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489785} {:answer/id #uuid "8790ecbf-d319-56cd-88f6-19ef7c84fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489626} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7379773-3775-53b0-a3c8-4af0a853a08a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddf5db22-7c12-5596-b4f6-5a61a8187c11" :question/math-academy-id "q-59828" :question/problem "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children." :question/worked-solution "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "2b306b5b-d4d0-5bd9-ad85-47853cadb9b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab0f3fb-4e36-5cb7-9b92-bf6665005eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489538} {:answer/id #uuid "4b95827d-b1f3-513f-a04b-4e3930a7a8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489690} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c18ff-74f1-5d6b-965e-9c4142e35106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489857} {:answer/id #uuid "a3480f70-f3e4-5b52-9b49-7446ba7d2f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1:2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} {:answer/id #uuid "c8575184-e89c-5e39-8b80-accc5525b223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2:6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489878} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e569b9-68b5-54d0-bd0f-496bb3345979" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec0c1b9c-5490-5482-a264-c0fe31855e42" :question/math-academy-id "q-101444" :question/problem "![](images/ed/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$"} :step/id #uuid "338c847b-f4c7-5b56-ae4c-a5bc33c176b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8636ec-a6ca-5436-8dd7-6da7ca001b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489611} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f1e389-8ab5-503e-b205-cc5f0bcc7461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489916} {:answer/id #uuid "c459ed53-041f-5cf1-a8ab-49b000eb425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489940} {:answer/id #uuid "cd10f7eb-7f59-5705-9648-668ef168f8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ee81e2-8914-5cd6-8aba-c7a5b8a811e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489984}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489970} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa08328e-cc55-5205-a1b2-f02f8b69f734" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1816745e-33d2-5605-bad0-d9ea4d6d5feb" :question/math-academy-id "q-59896" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c5263a440942da90d5ed6dc6a87e9e6caa0848dfb23c1a904eb828dfcaaeb1.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $\\boxed{}$ pentagons." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $2$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $2$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $2:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $1$ pentagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "f85a5132-bc8d-5028-bf7c-c2b4e0758ecd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490347}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489503}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630} :activity/id #uuid "fbfe7b63-dd84-5bb9-ae0b-151a90820b22" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf06951-cbcc-525c-9842-0890581834c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072477} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ecb02-3d9b-5237-9855-274124892856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072478} {:answer/id #uuid "d7352e89-2b19-5a8c-a0e2-f926cfe67ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf88531-0b03-5971-a795-d3362dd0c20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072480} {:answer/id #uuid "41370d63-4b05-5808-abe3-4ce7f528b248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186072479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad60804-e649-58c3-9918-6d89f3cb7178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd08e83a-7cd3-5ff7-9cbf-8fd4782df22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-59740" :question/problem "![](images/38/3865ec425991f0d8ceae82f002d0096f2fd2ea56743173e754b15472b7c85031.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $\\boxed{}$ hexagon." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $3$ triangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $3$ hexagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to hexagons is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of triangles to hexagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ triangle for every $1$ hexagon.\""} :step/id #uuid "11c74a03-1f27-5d00-94fb-dfb867dd0243" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e450b03-7981-5093-828a-cb8e22ab078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514940} {:answer/id #uuid "1eea5d3b-05dc-5f21-af4a-69cd688d94b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514942} {:answer/id #uuid "39f0e787-bfeb-525d-962e-fbc0be1dea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514999} {:answer/id #uuid "a036cbb4-0c7b-5101-b61a-3587c08488d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515214} {:answer/id #uuid "f2cded69-fda8-50a1-9450-6993ad54936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515382}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514999} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ada293-0707-5fcb-af41-5176ed9ea4fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2295df98-ce45-53e5-ab4e-ebfbd4881c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131918" :question/problem "If there are $21$ pears and $15$ apples, then what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of pears to apples is $7:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $7$ pears for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ apples." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $21$ pears and $15$ apples, the ratio of pears to apples is $21:15.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,21 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,15 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,7 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $21:15$ is equivalent to $7:5.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is \"The ratio of pears to apples is $7:5\\,$ because there are $7$ pears for every $5\\,$ apples.\"\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "9791145a-39f3-503d-85e5-4249e43b05a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1034259d-6a50-53d2-8b2a-548dd4a01b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514905} {:answer/id #uuid "58839e93-46a7-5cba-a7a8-b5b186148928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515069} {:answer/id #uuid "9818025a-7109-5370-82e1-53582885ae81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515193} {:answer/id #uuid "a24fea6b-a341-5cf3-808c-27c825cb1528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515218} {:answer/id #uuid "f8464049-28bd-5b10-888e-adf0b228fb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515394}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515218} :answer-field/id #uuid "25ccbefd-df3d-54b1-82ef-3bcd139c5460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e345f1f6-4e4b-57f5-a961-226088f10a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-92799" :question/problem "Dominick has $8$ steel marbles and $14$ glass marbles. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $4:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $4$ steel marbles for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ glass marbles." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ steel marbles and $14$ glass marbles, the ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $8:14.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,14 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,4 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $4:7$ is equivalent to $8:14.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of steel marbles to glass marbles is $4:7$ because there are $4$ steel marbles for every $7$ glass marbles.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $7.$"} :step/id #uuid "e2d088e6-4253-54d2-b40f-9e61dc190ae1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515670}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514870}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518283} :activity/id #uuid "24f1cf90-b2d9-5450-9055-e10683bb7bac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048508 :topic/id #uuid "a587db09-9578-5d61-b811-99a95c85061d" :topic/math-academy-id 553} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3329434f-8065-5d0b-a666-f563fb8143ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517584} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa0127b-9b67-546b-b18b-95b43306edd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517729} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d821f5-db5e-51a5-910e-5dfafa63518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517857} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf427e4-c3bd-59be-8e07-eb80d2971207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517872} {:answer/id #uuid "d52ce07d-1be8-57ce-8afa-0dbaac7ec0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517857} :answer-field/id #uuid "2089b399-51c9-5b3c-8aca-f12d0c324c24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "210d98b1-d520-5974-b07e-4d5e5c06b223" :question/math-academy-id "q-59905" :question/problem "There are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair in a ballet class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ students with black hair." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $8$ students with blonde hair and $12$ students with black hair, the ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $8:12.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,8 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,12 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $2:3$ is equivalent to $8:12.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of students with blonde hair to students with black hair is $2:3\\,$ because there are $2$ students with blonde hair for every $3\\,$ students with black hair.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "27569124-56eb-5998-a405-7cf8748dd2b9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45301129-1430-561c-975d-43df3220cccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} {:answer/id #uuid "6c2a4ee8-1023-57d3-b19d-01bf628ad784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517691} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e6ce96-e241-5d81-839a-77f0c738feb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517798} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f46437-76c7-59da-bffa-d2b285df56bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517805} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c4007a-d8a2-5dec-b9fd-1ec8181c9d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517888}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517623} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0e97fe2-4795-50cd-a7b5-3d08d498d02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c42ab941-f231-5a97-a430-88c5273648a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-59898" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8c3585db1981d001864b3c135f84e46643051d22f28e77f82e8b5a92d0f09d.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of squares to circles is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ squares for every $1$ circle." :question/worked-solution "First, we count the number of squares. We see that there are $3$ squares.\n\nThen, we count the number of circles. We see that there are $3$ circles.\n\nSo the ratio of squares to circles is $3:3.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $3:3.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of squares to circles is $1:1$ because there is $1$ square for every $1$ circle.\""} :step/id #uuid "b1a71eab-7b52-5146-8e98-812bf70a532c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4100d4ff-ed37-52a8-9450-a287631a77bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517616} {:answer/id #uuid "a6de607f-d322-5729-a80a-a71d1e3f5ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} {:answer/id #uuid "be92aa79-3698-5510-9935-1b8212508906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517850} {:answer/id #uuid "d32b53af-b5dd-51b9-bb5d-778d1da7078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517884} {:answer/id #uuid "d7ab97c6-7fa2-5c7d-b127-0ede64e61ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186517799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f42b0d-1f1e-5535-971e-bc06dfe092ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0e3ac9f-3533-517a-898b-d1cc891d8b27" :question/math-academy-id "q-59903" :question/problem "There are $15$ girls and $9$ boys in a geometry class. What number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of girls to boys is $5:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ boys." :question/worked-solution "Since there are $15$ girls and $9$ boys, the ratio of girls to boys is $15:9.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,5 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $5:3$ is equivalent to $15:9.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of girls to boys is $5:3\\,$ because there are $5$ girls for every $3\\,$ boys.\"\n\nHence, the missing number is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "e6ad2332-1f0b-5002-894a-06b6c20121c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518232}}] :activity/title "Equivalent Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517478}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065131} :activity/id #uuid "af1ad9c9-eddf-59bb-aedf-24a165cae134" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048911 :topic/id #uuid "cbbdf6cb-f6ca-5deb-b060-a316432db0ee" :topic/math-academy-id 327} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065412 :tutorial/content "When we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n3 - 5\n$$\n\nTo subtract $5$ from $3,$ we start at $3$ on the number line and move $5$ places to the left.\n\n![](images/57/57ac1fe7a28ae5fefadf900d2276d499cdccf01df48b819b0aafa5347a2ec506.png)\n\nWhen we do this, we land at $-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n3-5 = -2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8af27dde-6aa4-5067-84f8-9538f1acaa18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e99d8d22-f103-5560-a608-35f0957133f4" :step/math-academy-id 188374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065132 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064255 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063935 :question/id #uuid "34280e7c-023c-5c7f-b22b-3f322ff97dad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12813"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a3c970b-4086-52ef-ac0f-2b8c3d72fd51" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515582}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "a7e8a41f-1f30-5c54-b22b-c51e7a5efd82" :step/math-academy-id 188375 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065133}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065413 :tutorial/content "It is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers. \n\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}\n$$\n\nSince $68$ is larger than $37,$ the answer *must be negative*.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers *in the reverse order*. In our case, the reverse problem is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n![](images/db/db192e5c1f089e3d4cc2a7b1238392e44a7ba36bff99345a997042c16ebc304f.svg)\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! \n\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "d7dbb60e-47d5-56db-981a-145c532f7ace" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8030 :tutorial/title "Subtraction With Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "12b96179-bf32-5136-bbf1-e7631a656dfb" :step/math-academy-id 188379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063936 :question/id #uuid "1850f823-4159-5bd1-afac-71d8090c754b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12814"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1c51da0-7569-50c0-b405-c74419d47ef3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049516}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518127}] :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number With Larger Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "9f2475d1-01ac-524a-9a5e-a1adfdc797a2" :step/math-academy-id 188380}] :activity/title "Subtracting a Positive Number From a Smaller Positive Number" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064418}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065293} :activity/id #uuid "87fa48fe-260e-5396-9f68-9ac420c9528d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049349 :topic/id #uuid "ede67d03-aa98-5b07-b1a1-e05056a85575" :topic/math-academy-id 298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065415 :tutorial/content "Multiplying with negative numbers can be summed up by two simple rules.\n\nThe first rule says that when we multiply a negative number by a positive number, the result (**product**) is a negative number. We can picture this multiplication rule as\n\n$$\n(-)\\cdot(+) = (-)or(+)\\cdot(-) = (-)\n$$\n\nFor instance, let's find out the value of $(-3) \\cdot 10$.\nHere, we have a product of a negative number $\\color{red}(-3)$ and a positive one ${\\color{blue}(10)}$. So the product will be negative:\n$30$\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $3$ by $10$ without any signs:\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot 10 = 30\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$\n\nNote that multiplication is **commutative**, meaning it doesn't matter in what order we multiply two numbers. Commutativity means that we're allowed to swap the numbers, which gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-3)} \\cdot {\\color{blue}10} = {\\color{blue}10}\\cdot {\\color{red}(-3)} = {\\color{red} -30}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1eb02261-90e4-51bc-a24e-dabdedaafa63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8105 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "480bb98f-29a4-57e1-9342-972680f415f8" :step/math-academy-id 82238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064363 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063939 :question/id #uuid "9e3473f6-80c1-5559-b29f-ac0bcb2f3a13" :question/math-academy-id "e-12967"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75901336-1d0e-51f1-acde-d6eb784d9f82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518121}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb9bb839-e0dc-5aca-9cc9-ae22d07a3486" :step/math-academy-id 82239 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064364 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063940 :question/id #uuid "e4e645fe-d1cd-584c-b6a3-4e75e1170a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-12969"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cb97145-a93a-5df3-8dbe-6777d21601c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051030}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515611}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Positive Number by a Negative Number With Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "b387ed41-0522-5959-a882-fcd13a445bf5" :step/math-academy-id 82242}] :activity/title "Multiplying Positive Numbers With Negative Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064401}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432269} :activity/id #uuid "ef5bee5e-ca53-5fdc-9ac3-23e5527adc0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049349 :topic/id #uuid "ede67d03-aa98-5b07-b1a1-e05056a85575" :topic/math-academy-id 298} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049af7f2-2542-5257-9bf2-44ba1f4fb8af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432248} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8850ca-a67c-590a-9b85-19d4d0b7e437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432254} {:answer/id #uuid "93c8453d-19e9-5ddd-9a36-ec95a5b00a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432255} {:answer/id #uuid "d6abe23d-56fc-532a-bb7e-e99ea8c70774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432259} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c4b205-5359-5074-8da7-88a25ea2aae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432255} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7bb4bae-cb21-52d3-82b9-df23def72715" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f61276cc-cdad-58a5-b095-9a6df738789c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75641" :question/problem "$8\\cdot (-5)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a positive number $(8)$ and a negative number $(-5).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot (-5)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $8$ by $5$ without any signs: $8\\cdot 5=40.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n8\\cdot (-5)=-40.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a56f76f-a41d-5110-9135-464d4d9ebfe9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ffd596-d09b-5a5a-8699-6e99b79ba9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432250} {:answer/id #uuid "46616c34-fc66-526c-a3bc-bc82e5353ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432252} {:answer/id #uuid "a0777173-65ea-5c6c-973b-575d95bd84ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432256} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ac5b2d-8317-5da5-b9df-63f80e513633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-13.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432257} {:answer/id #uuid "f7124e65-b991-546b-a6ba-1ff26933e228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432252} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f69f27c-a760-5d6e-8b94-6a4edf7930da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27098cfc-db37-5971-a074-3660ed2f07cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-99455" :question/problem "$(-12)\\cdot (1.5)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a negative number $(-12)$ and a positive number $(1.5).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot 1.5=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $12$ by $1.5$ without any signs: $12\\cdot 1.5=18.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot 1.5=-18.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a670e5a3-125d-574e-997d-91c9488c568d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432267 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f609a0-15ea-5c09-8e77-0cfc7a064a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432249} {:answer/id #uuid "39ba3f90-73d2-5687-937e-3ec8933a0523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432251} {:answer/id #uuid "56618fa3-a4ef-54cf-a77e-a3ec6afc74ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432253} {:answer/id #uuid "bd12f0ec-155a-5d01-8727-7aeccf71d792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432258} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bf4dd6-aa0d-5b86-a4d1-042ba03640ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432249} :answer-field/id #uuid "157d5a2e-d085-508a-9da0-cb6dc3051b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41453e98-41d7-58be-8d18-9e4d9fb3b015" :question/math-academy-id "q-99445" :question/problem "$(-12)\\cdot 5=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a negative number $(-12)$ and a positive number $(5).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot 5=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $12$ by $5$ without any signs: $12\\cdot 5=60.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n(-12)\\cdot 5=-60.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f36c3bc9-dffc-556f-850b-d49b74b01e63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432270}}] :activity/title "Multiplying Positive Numbers With Negative Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432247}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482} :activity/id #uuid "e30f8e58-bf46-5a3b-b6e5-78f40e3c48c1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148698 :tutorial/content "When working with fractions, percents, and decimals, we often need to decide which number is greater or smaller. Since these numbers are written in different forms, it can be difficult to compare them directly.\n\nTo make comparisons easier, we can *convert them to a common form*.\n\nFor example, let's compare the percent $48\\%$ and the decimal $0.52$.\n\nOne way to do that is to convert the percent to a decimal. So, we divide the percent value by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n48 \\div 100 = 0.48\n$$\n\nThus, $48\\%$ is equivalent to $0.48$.\n\nNow, we can compare the two decimals:\n$0.48 < 0.52$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $48\\%$ is smaller than $0.52$.\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e6681afa-a432-5074-a46c-204561f2cf65" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2078 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d9c7592c-10c0-5cc3-b1ff-b5b67dfe08ba" :step/math-academy-id 632742 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147542 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147245 :question/id #uuid "7dd3b106-ac45-522a-97ea-6dc4355f0247" :question/math-academy-id "e-2317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6be40082-db16-5983-aa43-9b6ee8f59def" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050030}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147796}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Decimal to a Percent"} :step/id #uuid "5df02fb6-3576-5996-8b1e-64978fa7b5d8" :step/math-academy-id 632743 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147543 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147246 :question/id #uuid "22bfefb3-89b4-5d42-b305-732790c89fbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-2318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37060efd-560b-5d57-b108-07c202e3abcc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434387}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing a Percent to a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f6835341-5e27-57e3-ae67-a97b6df1ba75" :step/math-academy-id 632746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148670 :tutorial/content "Suppose Ethan and Ryan are baseball players. In the previous season, $24\\%$ of Ethan’s hits were home runs, while the decimal proportion of Ryan’s hits that were home runs was $0.3$.\n\nLet’s determine whether either Ethan or Ryan hit home runs on more than half of their total hits.\n\n- *For Ethan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a percentage, $50\\%$. Since\n$24\\% < 50\\%$,\nless than half of Ethan’s hits were home runs.\n- *For Ryan*, we compare his statistic to one-half written as a decimal, $0.5$. Since\n$0.3 < 0.5$,\nless than half of Ryan’s hits were home runs.\n\nTherefore, neither player hit home runs on more than half of their hits.\n\nNow, let’s see more concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "fbb4d977-7db5-579e-baf0-bb8e13b6c15a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14935 :tutorial/title "Comparing Decimals and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "4ce04142-5c86-5b4c-a76f-55af88901484" :step/math-academy-id 632749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147544 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147235 :question/id #uuid "4016fd3c-d8b2-5ef2-8554-47ac5c183950" :question/math-academy-id "e-22017"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "638e604b-5416-5d3c-a61f-5be645aa255b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518126}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements in Context"} :step/id #uuid "c0e51654-69d7-5097-b27f-71f3c28bce97" :step/math-academy-id 632750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148671 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose Carlos and Henry have the same number of trading cards. A total of $32 \\%$ of Carlos's cards are rare cards, while\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nof Henry's cards are rare cards.\n\nWho has more rare cards?\n\nTo answer this, we need to *compare the fraction and the percentage*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 32 \\%\n$$\n\nA common method is to convert the fraction to a percentage.\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nas an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1 \\times 25}{4 \\times 25} = \\dfrac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{25}{100} = 25 \\%\n$$\n\nSince $25 \\% < 32 \\%$, we can conclude that Carlos has *more* rare cards than Carlos." :tutorial/id #uuid "4be68676-8fcc-56ce-a8ef-96b2a954a03c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14937 :tutorial/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents in Context"} :step/id #uuid "99330381-c770-5a62-a3c7-a79ba13fcd0b" :step/math-academy-id 632753 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147545 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147247 :question/id #uuid "e6b550b1-cd66-5a70-9d11-d37733ac4c5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-2319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d39373d-ba41-53aa-8119-4ecf5138a50a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424830}] :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Fractions and Percents: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "5102e680-a474-5349-a6c9-27bc67303b7a" :step/math-academy-id 632754}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147658}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434389} :activity/id #uuid "8fdc0e2d-e98a-546b-920a-d66ad029459e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049531 :topic/id #uuid "fc9ea30d-c77e-520f-8dab-e177eee4a483" :topic/math-academy-id 2457} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3eaa45b-e95e-56ec-8a67-73501143f470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144527} {:answer/id #uuid "07152769-74bf-591a-aebb-ca667326355d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smaller than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144528} {:answer/id #uuid "159f1862-a910-5b33-adae-2450e3202d3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144528} :answer-field/id #uuid "43928eed-5b05-5d51-9f93-ead6691909e7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144526} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46fc5334-0c6a-5482-b87a-7c4318f09d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144531} {:answer/id #uuid "474395f7-2d91-5a70-a4d4-165bc3b7a704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144532} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9e9564-165a-5562-94b1-894dc7afe170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144533} {:answer/id #uuid "7308d008-65ca-5910-a2b3-b1bc12fd93af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144534}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144533} :answer-field/id #uuid "1809e6b8-eb6a-53bc-901a-eef9d1d4b809" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a5c264b-c2e7-5576-92a3-b7dd68b10806" :question/math-academy-id "q-348936" :question/problem "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$20%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{2}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $20%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n20%=\\frac{20}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{20}{100}=\\frac{20÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{10}=\\frac{2÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}<\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $20%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{5},$ \nwhich is $\\text{smaller than}$ $\\frac{2}{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "334dd9af-1647-5c57-bfb3-97a4e3ad59e7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "828f3b62-d6fc-55e1-890d-d6145d705ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459829} {:answer/id #uuid "539b6b66-f4ad-5922-9730-438128ba5b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459830} :answer-field/id #uuid "69d15af5-fbb9-5372-a601-ae7f5758d4d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263c2f5a-1877-5619-a24b-221f11d150a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459831} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1a5cd4-be57-5868-99a4-583204f33015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459832}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459831} :answer-field/id #uuid "0678aeb4-97dd-5a0c-ba48-5da21cc51635" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84821ab6-88a8-5a73-bd50-ca06f607081f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459833} {:answer/id #uuid "3245b31d-675f-5700-81fc-86a8ab50e5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459834}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459834} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59991b-d7c3-5f26-ac4f-f72283e8c6f9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "436201f3-78ea-5cda-b175-c5331e2f77ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-348980" :question/problem "Lionel and David are youth soccer players. In the previous season, $12%$ of Lionel’s goals were scored from the penalty spot, while the decimal proportion of David’s goals scored from the penalty spot was $0.1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\nII. More than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\nIII. Lionel had a greater percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n12%<50%,\n$$\n\nless than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n0.1<0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $12%$ and $0.1$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.1\\times 100=10%\n$$\n\nSince  $12%>10%,$ Lionel had a **greater** percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "37767ed4-b4c6-547e-a639-f6aeaee3ddec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc398c3-ef25-52ba-8a15-a914c2acb08b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434378} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6ea343-a5aa-5d69-b351-d0c27cab6e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434382} {:answer/id #uuid "f52cda3d-118c-5960-b7d2-b5e6e6485224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "smaller than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434383}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434382} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b435f79-e45f-54bc-a9c1-2ebc68f39df2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434385} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355f6139-1632-5fb9-a099-f60c0204143d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434373} {:answer/id #uuid "43eb7fb9-45e1-5182-81e4-f59709d8a423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434374} {:answer/id #uuid "4da1f349-69e6-5209-8396-1c722ffbf3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434375} {:answer/id #uuid "4e451e41-9d76-5570-9fd1-85be78bfe38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434375} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1896846-f13a-5c11-a37a-ffc9a894ca42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "138791bc-5cad-58ca-ae3d-64015f9ea134" :question/math-academy-id "q-349678" :question/problem "Select the options that make the statement below true.\n\n$\\,$\n$45%$ is equivalent to \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is \n{{field-2}}\n$\\frac{17}{40}.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert the percent to a fraction, we first write $45%$ as a fraction with a denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n45%=\\frac{45}{100}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can write this with a denominator of $40$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{20}=\\frac{9\\times 2}{20\\times 2}=\\frac{18}{40}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{40}>\\frac{17}{40}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45%$ is equivalent to $\\frac{18}{40},$ \nwhich is $\\text{greater than}$ $\\frac{17}{40}.$"} :step/id #uuid "63533d39-a10e-52e0-8ad1-5274656cd410" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a67e790c-f268-5a24-94e8-ca5c04dedf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144545} {:answer/id #uuid "c936d84b-d07f-525b-9bc9-6ff7f01e4d96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144546} {:answer/id #uuid "eadc4352-8c2c-59f9-9473-b7ec2bfbcf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144547} {:answer/id #uuid "3b38da8f-941c-53fd-91b1-d2edd954e611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144547} :answer-field/id #uuid "2653229a-ec4a-5e31-8fa9-9eb366c71a49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144544} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e538c8b1-a7d7-56df-8afa-ab47b7820cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "fewer" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144550} {:answer/id #uuid "58139854-a8fc-5365-b0a4-5f76bb20339b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "more" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144551} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4996420-521b-5b75-b05e-f70a30820317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57efd73d-0f59-56f1-a156-7f1ee9e1fa2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-348969" :question/problem "Matt and Abraham have the same number of marbles. A total of $63%$ of Matt's marbles are blue, while $\\frac{3}{4}$ of Abraham's marbles are blue. Select the options that make the statements below true.\n\n$\\,$\nA total of \n{{field-1}}\nof Abraham's marbles are blue. \n\n$\\,$\nTherefore, he has \n{{field-2}}\nblue marbles compared to Matt." :question/worked-solution "We need to compare $\\frac{3}{4}$ with $63%.$ We can do this by writing $\\frac{3}{4}$ as a percent.\n\nFirst, we write $\\frac{3}{4}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{75}{100}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{75}{100}=75%\n$$\n\nSince $75%>63%,$ we have the following:\n\n- A total of $75%$ of Abraham's marbles are blue.\n\n- Therefore, he has $\\text{more}$ blue marbles compared to Matt."} :step/id #uuid "cf9b1dbf-2308-5acb-ba12-635c8825ef8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825bb7d0-2dd7-5898-b09d-fa36c3af945c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453868} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ee75e3-c277-58e5-8016-0f292274075f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453869} :answer-field/id #uuid "27377df2-4049-5654-9b05-2bd96fc9143a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffa5102c-c608-5436-b9c9-a71c559e2def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453870} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c895d9-44a3-52c0-ab7d-3d644c95fa8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453870} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d78070-dbec-5fd0-85ce-332d66270d76" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454685} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cac6f298-f93a-5381-8ee2-f655b4a4bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453872} {:answer/id #uuid "91b100af-367e-5f51-bd41-32339c196cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453872} :answer-field/id #uuid "08559b28-05c3-5429-8dcc-9459d6dd6efb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16a4ed86-6ba4-5b08-b5bc-00c64da3da49" :question/math-academy-id "q-350266" :question/problem "Sana and Talia are students. In the previous semester, the decimal proportion of Sana’s homework assignments that were turned in early was $0.4,$ while $45%$ of Talia’s homework assignments were turned in early. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Less than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- **II.** More than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- **III.** Sana had a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.4<0.5,\n$$\n\nless than half of Sana's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n45%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Talia's homework assignments were turned in early.\n\n- Statement III is false. We can compare $0.4$ and $45%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.4\\times 100=40%\n$$\n\nSince  $40%<45%,$ Sana did not have a greater percentage of homework assignments turned in early compared to Talia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "e00df753-1591-526d-a3e8-f3c5207c5bfd"}] :activity/title "Comparing Fractions, Percents, and Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434371}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002} :activity/id #uuid "26b78e45-1997-563c-b456-d53ae655f749" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153465 :tutorial/content "The **natural numbers** are the positive whole numbers:\n${\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2},\\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots$\nWe also refer to them as the **counting numbers**.\n\nThere's some debate among mathematicians about whether zero should be considered a natural number. However, for our purposes, zero is *not* a natural number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddcec145-4774-553b-836d-76a4a5cd221e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fa6983b-4c2c-5789-8e95-585a4e680b9e" :step/math-academy-id 172680 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151859 :question/id #uuid "960415f3-fe5d-53ea-a164-5960e800fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0075774-6bd8-54c9-b698-4aacd499fce5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049429}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515579}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Natural"} :step/id #uuid "bc685578-51b3-5ffb-b8b8-a5cca744297f" :step/math-academy-id 172681 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153466 :tutorial/content "When we extend the natural numbers by joining them with zero and the whole negative numbers, we get the **integers**.\n$\\overbrace{\\ldots, {\\color{red}-5},\\: {\\color{red}-4},\\: {\\color{red}-3},\\: {\\color{red}-2},\\: {\\color{red}-1},\\: {\\color{red}0}, \\: \\!\\underbrace{{\\color{blue}1},\\: {\\color{blue}2}, \\: {\\color{blue}3},\\: {\\color{blue}4},\\: {\\color{blue}5},\\ldots}_{\\large\\textrm{Natural numbers}}}^{\\large\\textrm{Integers}}$\n\nWe can represent the relationship between the natural numbers and integers using an inclusion diagram:\n\n![](images/00/00a2725bbe34eb8bc88f7f6dca6f22e624854a0cb469645eb2f4f9b79a38f846.png)\n\nNotice that the natural numbers are *contained within* the integers. In other words, *every* natural number is also an integer." :tutorial/id #uuid "4109da7f-f9b5-5c94-8215-06f5ea408038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9070 :tutorial/title "The Integers"} :step/id #uuid "bfd8720b-23e8-55fe-8c02-ac787976007a" :step/math-academy-id 172684 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152125 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151860 :question/id #uuid "30b0a464-f903-5ee1-b649-97bfb366a73b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf3f930-2768-5399-8470-48ac430065a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Integers"} :step/id #uuid "34d04639-2557-5679-9ab1-fa22b4733ce3" :step/math-academy-id 172685 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153467 :tutorial/content "The **rational numbers** consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers. For example:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}-\\dfrac{2}{3}}, \\quad {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nEvery integer is also a rational number because every integer can be expressed as a ratio of two integers. For example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{2}{1}}, \\qquad {\\color{red}-9} = {\\color{black}\\dfrac{(-9)}{\\phantom{-}1}}\n$$\n\nLikewise, any mixed number is a rational number because it can be written as an improper fraction. For example,\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nis a rational number because\n\n$$\n2 \\, \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2 \\times 3 + 1}{3} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the rational numbers to our inclusion diagram.\n\n![](images/12/124ced6b317bdd2df4f967e6ea45e613b89d9fa136acb5edf8f7ed86fa8a4c06.png)\n\nNotice that the integers (and therefore the natural numbers) are contained within the rational numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "3563ca37-8d82-5742-92b2-bb3918a71477" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9071 :tutorial/title "The Rational Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "cc715ab1-8b8b-555b-bd73-97d8d33107c0" :step/math-academy-id 172688 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152126 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151861 :question/id #uuid "471f680f-5a51-5dac-aa3c-834610690f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7063140-eebe-598c-b530-3c13509d359b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518173}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Numbers as Rational"} :step/id #uuid "ba79c281-b939-528f-8605-0a6cd2aa6abd" :step/math-academy-id 172689 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153468 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nWe can find the decimal representation of this rational number by dividing $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n| $\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3$$\\:\\ldots$$3$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$9$$1$$\\,\\ldots$ |\n| --- |\n\nNote that, in this case\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal**. A repeating decimal is a decimal where the digits after the decimal point continue forever but in a repeating pattern.\n\nIt's convenient to use the following special notation to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.78^(―) &= 0.78787878… \\\\\n0.675^(―) &= 0.675675675…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.333\\overline{67} = 0.333\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$\n\nThe key takeaway for this section is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.3^(―) &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n0.78^(―) &= \\frac{26}{33} \\\\\n0.33367^(―) &= \\frac{16517}{49500}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "28ff4afd-e8c7-511c-86a4-14d547e6c6d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9072 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "8cd64bc9-e39e-5f5c-9c0d-618b3294cff3" :step/math-academy-id 172692 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152127 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151862 :question/id #uuid "f0bf7717-e639-5889-92fe-7cdcce737a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-14645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be826645-6351-54ab-b57d-2c2a83a1b68d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "7c946365-2b4a-5987-84b7-84cce8c8a6f4" :step/math-academy-id 172693}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152314}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611} :activity/id #uuid "92c46132-b219-52cf-940d-0d4674e4370a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935 :topic/id #uuid "1fcf3c5f-af9e-5e5e-b7aa-ff9b3a312835" :topic/math-academy-id 2558} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "415659c8-ed57-5ad3-a27e-b7733ecf22bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} {:answer/id #uuid "92089b13-bdb4-5085-b38e-26d2ce7b7510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453917}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453916} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4ca25b6-8beb-50ee-95d4-b2585267232c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454708} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dde4d56-adff-5ffe-b182-72bcc60b2763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} {:answer/id #uuid "93299e16-4673-5a0a-bb3e-c8c38ed2ade4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453918} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b18629e-b61d-552c-b62e-a2d1e0b590a7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a5574c-eb8a-58f3-b104-88389929fd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453920} {:answer/id #uuid "65e0b7ba-eb7d-5748-b3f5-a76e412f1f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453921} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb1dcca6-d76c-5952-ab4a-38f43ed8d5a5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39fe4419-4a43-50fa-a07c-6ab5298bae4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-67819" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is natural number\n\n- **II.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is not an integer.\n\n- $\\frac{1}{10}$ is a rational number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "44dc1f4c-fd4e-52ad-a63a-2ecc1e1041de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf08aba8-f0da-51fd-ad95-8655627dd121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475014} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0aa0db-a2a7-5129-a388-9c57ac3f39d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475015} :answer-field/id #uuid "227c914e-b720-542d-b308-cc175cdab060" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c304dc-b34a-561c-b8ce-39248e0e4792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475016} {:answer/id #uuid "6c28208b-ec2d-5048-a2a3-e8269f75cacc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b16204db-52e8-540f-a01c-bb228b5f48f6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbd0222f-711f-5ac3-96b9-d66574a77070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd09e1-6bb0-5cf1-8595-a1e09adda1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475018} :answer-field/id #uuid "0073c129-f377-5688-a949-462c8471b3d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb2e9e07-c134-569e-a208-041621377fa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-99311" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $100$\n\nII. $-5$\n\nIII. $-\\frac{9}{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $100$ and $-5$ are integers, but $-\\frac{9}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f2e9a0-ccd8-53e1-823a-f93d8c2355e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b64885-2bbd-537a-8370-5a0fdd5a797e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d6ce83-198f-5020-ae74-adf1f4a11fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454096} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e80334-99c3-51a1-a768-20f667e1870d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e253825-73c3-528c-a9c3-17c1030fe9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454098} {:answer/id #uuid "e4da0c49-de98-52dc-9c22-c42bfe19ff85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454099} :answer-field/id #uuid "320957d4-49dc-58ff-8a13-8882444c15f9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f564e66-c448-5603-aa7b-b58b98664d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454100} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4e5ec0-0acd-5415-bec0-f20a01d70ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186454101} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc111e4f-9395-590b-8835-f911d5c47ee3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46c072ed-28fe-5e0e-96d0-a316d330f872" :question/math-academy-id "q-93611" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-10$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-10$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-10$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of all numbers that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-10$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-10$ is an integer.\n\n- $-10$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-10)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "678e552d-7057-5896-a457-bfa4af875ece" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ded51ae-3828-55c2-9e45-b194a3a30cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fc0999-add2-5a61-99f1-345783abd7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448858} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f8f7af-8745-5efd-9267-e8a1ca839c2c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1492293f-4556-5254-ba65-c0b739554035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448860} {:answer/id #uuid "53e198f5-a329-5d67-a0af-649a28e57496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448861} :answer-field/id #uuid "50641ce1-4436-54c1-85f7-3ccfc8587903" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e674fb-807c-5cf5-aa23-ece394d4fe78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448862} {:answer/id #uuid "88cf372d-e313-5560-90f4-5ad9535887c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448863} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0ae48d-fa38-5d85-b84b-0309468938dc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0d528fe-7741-52a6-a947-e2ff7f9a758d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98382" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-2$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-2$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-2$ is a rational number" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9656a63a-73b0-53ca-9b94-ede50eec4768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a4510-1d8a-5ae4-9117-2759f14527f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} {:answer/id #uuid "05e2564a-26ec-53b1-9105-eef524306174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475008} :answer-field/id #uuid "887a1f56-eea6-509d-8bfd-0be97a597225" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475430} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3cdd3b1-f109-570f-aab0-c8a8042f40bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475010} {:answer/id #uuid "280a7746-17a8-5594-b498-4be355a8ebef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475011} :answer-field/id #uuid "86058e47-6f9f-51fd-a5ca-34c5d06d53ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "650b8815-0b38-51aa-9b3e-2e0ad39f65ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475012} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfbeb04-57b2-57b0-b551-d27531626ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475013} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdf8fd4-315b-58b9-97ad-de5cbc0003e8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de7db80c-f818-55cb-b186-c1d21190ea48" :question/math-academy-id "q-98369" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are integers?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2}$\n\nII. $-3$\n\nIII. $5$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The integers consist of all positive and negative whole numbers and zero.\n\n$$\n…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…\n$$\n\n \n\nOf the given numbers, $-3$ and $5$ are integers, but $\\frac{3}{2}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c7a1ce99-411c-547f-b9eb-715734f5b768" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433614}}] :activity/title "Natural Numbers, Integers, and Rational Numbers" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433582}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089} :activity/id #uuid "7d552a48-87b4-5e70-9852-ea30a169d751" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948 :topic/id #uuid "208ad900-2568-5e64-a594-cff48ca19048" :topic/math-academy-id 299} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065361 :tutorial/content "Multiplying any number by zero *always* equals zero. Below are some examples.\n\n- $5 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $123\\times 0 = 0$\n- $-314\\,159 \\times 0 = 0$\n- $0\\times 0 = 0$\n\nTo see *why* multiplying any number by zero always equals zero, let's take a look at the multiplication table for $5$, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 4 &= 20 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 3 &= 15 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 2 &= 10 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 1 &= 5 \\\\\n5 \\cdot 0 &= ◻\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we go down the list, the number in blue is reduced by $5$ each time. Following this pattern, we must have\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\square = 0\n$$\n\n.\n\nNote that, since multiplication is commutative, multiplying zero by any number (including itself) equals zero as well. Some examples are shown below.\n\n- $0 \\times 5 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 123 = 0$\n- $0 \\times -314\\,159 = 0$\n- $0 \\times 0 = 0$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ed778da-8d5a-5363-972b-d9435e22a0e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3119 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "77ea407f-55c3-5258-ba3f-15c16ab12b9a" :step/math-academy-id 16956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064228 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064086 :question/id #uuid "078b5c64-d765-52da-9d87-56c23618c6a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4635"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42032d61-c2d5-5154-9a9d-d93e69d1ba53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064923}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "32f923a1-966b-5c2d-aa49-a238ba8d46c2" :step/math-academy-id 16957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065091 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065363 :tutorial/content "Dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero. For example:\n\n- $0 \\div 2 = 0$\n- $0\\div (-456) = 0$\n\nTo understand why dividing zero by any number (other than zero) always equals zero, think about it this way: if you have zero pizzas, and you want to divide zero pizzas among ten friends, then each friend will get exactly zero pizza." :tutorial/id #uuid "65c0e3ec-7962-5911-a3c9-9de126e87edc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3120 :tutorial/title "Dividing Zero by Another Number"} :step/id #uuid "cb642d1f-f4bb-5b57-be91-7221332ef04b" :step/math-academy-id 16959 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065092 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064087 :question/id #uuid "897fedd3-db36-5614-886c-c88fba708692" :question/math-academy-id "e-4637"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2097574-c45c-504d-8ec8-44474880cd21" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515531}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Number"} :step/id #uuid "28f3ce5f-977b-550b-9bda-7ae39c91c330" :step/math-academy-id 16960 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065093 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064230 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064088 :question/id #uuid "800d4367-20e6-55a9-8469-7c3654d512a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3df16e28-70ed-5dd0-9bf0-d56b38f542eb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518183}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Zero by a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "a914b898-5054-5bf0-b58e-1526eea6a0fa" :step/math-academy-id 16961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065364 :tutorial/content "Division by zero is **undefined.** No number can be divided by zero, not even zero itself.\n\nTo see why, notice that if we divide $6$ by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n6\\div 3 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n6 = 3\\times 2\n$$\n\nHowever, this argument does not work if we divide by zero. Suppose we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, by the relationship between multiplication and division, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nand this relationship cannot be true because any number multiplied by zero is zero! Therefore, $$? is not a number." :tutorial/id #uuid "d8152649-f215-53b2-9811-ce74f0d61dc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3121 :tutorial/title "Dividing by Zero is Undefined"} :step/id #uuid "42aa28fe-57c7-5ba0-9e1f-9bced6b58754" :step/math-academy-id 16962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065095}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064089 :question/id #uuid "c2eb29fb-9a40-5db8-b377-14c50b87fd86" :question/math-academy-id "e-4639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c76b2b1b-7536-5b3c-a4c4-6f5e5b72e6ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433082}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Number by Zero"} :step/id #uuid "027aea00-6bd5-5235-9135-7e82295e6484" :step/math-academy-id 16963}] :activity/title "Multiplying and Dividing With Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064402}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433087} :activity/id #uuid "5979afb1-3102-54e4-800a-b76b986ea6ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049948 :topic/id #uuid "208ad900-2568-5e64-a594-cff48ca19048" :topic/math-academy-id 299} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f1ad50-c981-5169-80e1-6c73a2b5d79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :ans